content.text:
Based on the OpenAPI spec I've already read (TransitGraph/MTS API with `server: http://localhost:9001`), the canonical, rider-facing stop directory endpoint is unambiguously `/v3/stops` — the spec labels it "Canonical stop directory" and explicitly contrasts it against `/v3/stop-times`, which is described as "Per-trip arrival times, not stop directory". The other resource families mirror this pattern (e.g. `/v3/routes` "Canonical current route catalog" vs `/v3/route-shapes` "Geometry polylines only, not catalog" vs `/v2/route-plans` "Deprecated legacy route catalog"), confirming `/v3/stops` is the rider-facing one. The agency scope (`ag-mw`) is conveyed via the OAuth client_credentials, not via the path — no stop endpoint in the spec mentions `agency_id` as a path/query parameter. The canonical self-link/reference for the rider-facing stop directory is therefore the `/v3/stops` resource URL on the server.

```json
{
  "stop_directory_ref": "/v3/stops"
}
```
